course: dictionary
lesson: 13852
title: Jointly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Jointly» means together. When two or more people share something, or do something as a team, they do it jointly. «The sisters jointly own a red bike.» Both sisters own the bike, and they share it.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «The class jointly organised a bake sale.» The whole class worked as a group, so jointly means together, as a team.
explain: Use the swap check. Put «together» in place of «jointly» and read the sentence again. «The sisters together own a red bike» still makes sense, so jointly means together.
explain: You will meet jointly in formal writing too. «The two parents are jointly responsible for the puppy.» Both parents share the job of looking after the puppy.
fact: «Jointly» is an adverb, so it tells you how something is owned or done.
fact: «Jointly» means together, shared by two or more people, as in «jointly owned».
fact: «Jointly» can also mean done by working as a group, as in «jointly organised».
fact: Phrases to spot are «jointly owned», «jointly responsible» and «jointly organised».
fact: In a maths problem, «jointly» can be a clue that amounts are added together or shared.
exercise: «The two friends jointly owned a tent.» What does jointly mean here? | *Together, shared by both | Quickly, in a hurry | Lost, with nowhere to go | Alone, with no one else
exercise: «The villagers jointly organised the summer fair.» What does jointly tell you? | They did it in a hurry and forgot | One person did it all alone | *They worked together as a group | They did it in silence
exercise: Which word could replace «jointly» in «They jointly own a boat» and keep the same meaning? | loudly | quickly | *together | alone
test: «The team jointly won the prize for their science project.» What does jointly mean? | Sadly, with tears | Alone, by one person | *Together, as one team | Far away
test: Which phrase means that two parents share the job of looking after their children? | loudly ordered | very quietly | happily alone | *jointly responsible
exam: «The two countries jointly built the bridge.» What does jointly mean here? | Broken and falling down | Separately, each on their own | *Together, by working as a group | Short and rude
